# Mirage Source Remastered, rebuilt on Core as a world's own rules.
#
# This file is the engine's door. Every function the engine calls is here, and each one hands the
# work to the model that owns it. Nothing else lives here.
#
# Two folders sit below it, split by who writes what.
#
#   declarations/   the record models a world author fills in through the editor
#   behavior/       the rules, which nobody authors
#
#   declarations/character.cm   a class, and the kit it opens with
#   declarations/magic.cm       a spell, who may learn it, and what a class already knows
#   declarations/gear.cm        this game's own fields on the engine's items, and who may wield them
#   declarations/quests.cm      a quest, what finishing it takes, and who may take it
#   declarations/places.cm      this game's own fields on the engine's maps
#   declarations/beasts.cm      this game's own fields on the engine's creatures
#   declarations/territory.cm   this game's own fields on the engine's map groups
#
#   behavior/stats.cm      the four stats, the pool curves, and the experience table
#   behavior/sheet.cm      the keys the engine carries, and what a new character starts with
#   behavior/vitals.cm     the three pools, and what puts back into them
#   behavior/combat.cm     what a swing is worth, and what a body turns aside
#   behavior/fight.cm      where that lands on somebody
#   behavior/duel.cm       two players, and who is allowed to fight whom
#   behavior/death.cm      what dying costs, and where you wake up
#   behavior/places.cm     what a map means to a fight
#   behavior/beasts.cm     what a creature is made of, on top of how it walks
#   behavior/classes.cm    applying the class they chose, and what it is worth afterwards
#   behavior/spells.cm     what casting costs, and what a cast is worth
#   behavior/book.cm       what a character knows, has ready, and does with it
#   behavior/gear.cm       who may wield what, and how gear wears out
#   behavior/loot.cm       what a kill is worth to the people who made it
#   behavior/quests.cm     taking one, counting toward it, and handing it back
#   behavior/guilds.cm     a guild's level, its wars, and who may fight whom
#   behavior/ledger.cm     what a guild is charged, and what it is paid
#   behavior/perks.cm      what a guild is worth to the people in it
#   behavior/valor.cm      the war currency
#   behavior/guildquest.cm a guild's standing quest, and what finishing it pays
#   behavior/staff.cm      what somebody staging the world can force rather than wait for
#   behavior/calendar.cm   the calendar, worked out from the clock
#   behavior/season.cm     which season it is, and when the next one starts
#   behavior/territory.cm  land a guild holds, and what holding it is worth
#   behavior/contest.cm    war night, and the fight over a territory
#   behavior/levels.cm     experience, and what it buys
#   behavior/chat.cm       the channels this world’s own lines read on
#
# The original is a .NET solution of some hundreds of files. What is reproduced here is the game —
# its stats, its pools, its fights, its classes — on an engine that has never heard of any of it.

shared model Rules

    # Declaring runs once, before the world exists, so everything it says is about the game itself.
    public function Configure(Builder game)
        Chat.Declare(game);
        Sheet.Declare(game);
        Vitals.Declare(game);
        Fight.Declare(game);
        Classes.Declare(game);
        Book.Declare(game);
        Quests.Declare(game);
        Guilds.Declare(game);
        GuildQuest.Declare(game);
        Contest.Declare(game);
        Staff.Declare(game);

        # Four slots, as the original had: three potion keys and one spare. The keys are 1 to 4.
        game.HotkeyBar(4);

        # Once a second. A tick is a tenth of a second, so ten of them is the beat everything below
        # is written against - see Vitals.TickHundredths, which has to agree with this.
        game.TickEvery(10);
    end function

    # Somebody who has been here before keeps what they had; their values were saved with their
    # character. A first arrival is enrolled.
    public function OnPlayerJoined(Player who)
        Sheet.Enroll(who);

        # ⚠ Enrolling only runs for somebody who has never been here. A character made before
        # the experience bar existed carries no ceiling for it, and a meter with no ceiling is not
        # drawn, so the bar would be missing for every character that already existed.
        Levels.Mark(who);

        # Here as well as on the tick, or the guild flag is unwritten for the first two seconds and
        # the screen it gates is refused to a member who just logged in.
        Guilds.Show(who);
    end function

    # The world's own beat, once rather than once per body. What belongs to the world and to nobody in
    # it is settled here; anything about a person is below.
    public function OnTick()
        Season.Tick();
        Contest.Tick();
        Beasts.Turn();
        Beasts.Volley();
    end function

    # Once for each body in the world, every twentieth tick.
    public function OnPlayerTick(Player who)
        Vitals.Rest(who);
        Death.Tick(who);
        Staff.Show(who);
        Sheet.Show(who);
        Guilds.Tick(who);
        Guilds.ShowWars(who);
        Ledger.Tick(who);
        GuildQuest.Tick(who);
        Contest.Watch(who);
    end function

    # `on` names whoever the verb was used on, and is blank for one offered on a square. `picked` is
    # the line of a panel's list they had selected, and is blank for a verb reached any other way.
    public function OnAction(Player who, string action, string on, integer map, integer x, integer y,
                             string picked)
        if action == Fight.Attack
            Fight.Strike(who, map, x, y);
        end if

        # The engine aims this one: `on` is whoever they had targeted, themselves included. A spell
        # turned inward restores rather than lands, which is this game's rule about its own magic and
        # not the engine's about its aiming.
        if action == Book.Cast
            # A page named with it - from the book's own list, or from a bar slot bound there - is
            # prepared first, so casting THAT spell is one press rather than two. Named nothing, it
            # throws whatever is already prepared, which is the key Q has always done.
            integer page = Book.Numbered(picked);

            if page > 0
                Book.Prepare(who, page);
            end if

            if on == who.Name
                Book.Inward(who);
            end if

            if on != who.Name
                Book.Hurl(who, map, x, y);
            end if
        end if

        if action == Quests.Offer
            Quests.Ask(who, map, x, y);
        end if

        if action == Quests.ReadVerb
            Quests.Read(who, picked);
        end if

        if action == Quests.TakeVerb
            Quests.Accept(who, picked);
        end if

        if action == Quests.DropVerb
            Quests.Abandon(who, picked);
        end if

        if action == Book.PrepareVerb
            Book.Prepare(who, Book.Numbered(picked));
        end if

        if action == Book.ForgetVerb
            Book.Forget(who, Book.Numbered(picked));
        end if

        if action == Quests.Hand
            Quests.Give(who, map, x, y);
        end if

        # Named by the line they picked off the wars list, which carries the guild it is against.
        if action == Guilds.PeaceVerb
            Guilds.Peace(who, picked);
        end if

        # The two page verbs open on the client and tell the server nothing worth acting on. They
        # arrive anyway, because a verb is a verb; there is simply nothing to do about them.

        if action == GuildQuest.AcquireVerb
            GuildQuest.Acquire(who);
        end if

        if action == GuildQuest.AbandonVerb
            GuildQuest.Abandon(who);
        end if

        if action == Contest.ClaimVerb
            Contest.Claim(who);
        end if

        # ⚠ Staging, and the engine has already refused any of these to a body whose Staff.Is is
        # not set - the same condition the button is hidden by. Nothing here re-checks it, because a
        # verb's condition is checked on the server as well as drawn on the client.
        if action == Staff.NightVerb
            Staff.Night(who);
        end if

        if action == Staff.HurryVerb
            Staff.Hurry(who);
        end if

        if action == Staff.CloseVerb
            Staff.Close(who);
        end if

        if action == Staff.SettleVerb
            Staff.Settle(who);
        end if

        if action == Staff.SeasonVerb
            Staff.Season(who);
        end if
    end function

    # Typed at the server's own console, and only there. Nothing a player can reach: the engine hands
    # this handler the words it did not recognize itself, after its own commands have had them.
    public string function OnConsole(string command, string rest)
        yield Staff.FromConsole(command, rest);
    end function

    # Asked BEFORE anything happens, so a reason here stops the use outright.
    public string function OnMayUse(Player who, integer item, integer slot)
        yield Gear.Judge(who, item);
    end function

    # And afterwards, once the engine has done its own half. A scroll and a draught are this game's.
    public function OnItemUsed(Player who, integer item, integer slot)
        Book.Read(who, item);
        Vitals.Drink(who, item);
    end function

    # The Prepare button on the spellbook. A form rather than a verb per page: twenty verbs would be
    # twenty declarations saying the same thing.
    public function OnMessage(Player who, string message, Values values)
        if message == "Training"
            Levels.Train(who, values.Number("strength"), values.Number("defense"),
                         values.Number("speed"), values.Number("intelligence"));
            yield;
        end if

        if message == "Donation"
            Valor.Donate(who, values.Number("amount"));
            yield;
        end if

        if message == "Declaration"
            Guilds.War(who, values.Text("guild"));
        end if
    end function

    # A creature is in the world. Everything a fight reads off one is written here, because the engine
    # spawns a body carrying a copy of its record and has never heard of health.
    public function OnNpcSpawned(Npc it)
        Beasts.Wake(it);
    end function

    # A creature reached somebody it was chasing — or, for one that keeps its distance, reached the
    # gap it wanted and stopped there.
    public function OnContact(Npc it, Player who)
        Fight.Meet(it, who);
    end function

    # And one that reached another creature. Two handlers rather than one: a rule handed the wrong
    # kind of body runs and gives a wrong answer, where one never called at least gives none.
    public function OnNpcContact(Npc it, Npc other)
        Fight.MeetNpc(it, other);
    end function

    # A point of stamina a step, and an empty bar is a walk rather than a stop.
    public string function OnMayRun(Player who)
        yield Vitals.MayRun(who);
    end function

    public function OnRan(Player who)
        Vitals.Ran(who);
    end function

    # They took a step under their own power. A body low enough on blood leaves some of it behind.
    public function OnPlayerMoved(Player who, integer fromX, integer fromY)
        Fight.Bleed(who);
    end function

    # Nothing in this game refuses a death. Its refusals are asked earlier and are about who may be
    # fought at all, which is duel.cm - a murderer's minute of grace included.
    public string function OnMayDie(Player who, string cause)
        yield "";
    end function

    # And what one costs, asked while the body is still on the tile it fell on. `killer` is nobody
    # when the world itself did it; IsHere answers that.
    public function OnDied(Player who, Player killer, string cause)
        Death.Fell(who, killer, cause);
    end function

    # They got up, and are standing where they came back to. The original filled the three pools here
    # rather than at the moment of death, so a body on the floor is lying there empty.
    public function OnRose(Player who)
        Death.Rose(who);
    end function

    # One line of a dead creature's table, before it is rolled. Raised once per line, so a creature
    # carrying three things raises it three times.
    public function OnLoot(Spoils drop)
        Loot.Shed(drop);
    end function

end model
